pope francis has harshly criticized  europe for what he says is the  exploitation of migrants for political  purposes the pontiff was speaking during  his visit to a migrant camp on the greek  island of lesbos the island is a main  entry point for migrants from africa and  the middle east seeking asylum in europe  [Music]  a warm welcome for the pontiff as he  greets hundreds of migrants here on  lesbos  [Music]  he condemned the situation on the island  calling it a shipwreck of civilization  with deep regret we must admit that this  country like others  continues to be hard-pressed  and that in europe there are those who  persist in treating the problem  as if it doesn't concern them  this is tragic  he criticized the global attitude  towards and treatment of asylum seekers  it's easy to stir a public opinion by  instilling fear of others  yet why do we fail to speak up about the  exploitation of the poor  the remote causes of migration should be  attacked not the poor people who pay the  consequences and are even used for  political propaganda  just over 2 000 migrants live in the  camps here  a fraction of the numbers that crowded  the island just two years ago  but still there is little shelter from  the cold and strict restrictions on  people's movements  the pope's visit sparks hope for those  enduring these bleak conditions  and he came to visit us and not only  like hearing it from far but he came to  the field to see how we live to see how  things happen here so it's it's give us  hope and strength knowing that such a  leader thinking about us and coming to  visit us  pope francis has consistently warned  countries against turning their backs on  migrants and ignoring their plight  those here on lesbos are hoping his  message might finally now be heard  and in recent months the shortfalls of  eu's border policy have been brought  sharply into focus by belarus that's  where thousands of migrants have arrived  hoping to cross into eu member poland  brussels says the belarusian government  created the crisis by alluring the  migrants with false promises but some  say poland and the eu are ignoring human  suffering dwe's alexander fonnaman  reports  searching deep in the forest for people  in need of help as temperatures drop  well below freezing by night  a resident from a local village brought  us to a spot just outside the exclusion  zone where migrants were camping earlier  that day  people are dying  bodies are being found in the forest  but nobody talks about that  not long ago a friend saw a body by the  side of the road  he says he's ashamed and angry with the  way the government has been handling the  situation a storehouse a few kilometers  away the arta is one of many volunteers  trying to help  she tells me the migrants she meets in  the woods have been pushed back into  belarus multiple times  they want to warm up at least for a  moment they want to shave wash  themselves  they ask us whether there's a house  where they can hide or sleep for a bit  unfortunately  we cannot give them that  is particularly disappointed with the  role the european union is playing in  this crisis  my impression is that the european union  supports poland by giving them their  blessing for  but that's what's been happening in  poland the government changed the law to  legalize pushbacks officials argue the  current situation requires changes to  long-standing practices  the international asylum regulations and  in particular the geneva convention were  written in the 1950s  long before we had to deal with what  alexander lukashenko is doing  to send off criticism authorities have  promised more transparency although they  formally lifted the state of emergency  last week restrictions remain in place  no one without a special permit is  allowed to access the area the situation  at the polish border with belarus seems  to have calmed down a bit in recent days  apparently the number of migrants in the  area has decreased at least for now but  the border zone stays off limits for  visitors including journalists with  security forces on high alert  authorities don't seem to be willing to  open up the area anytime soon even  though humanitarian organizations are  raising the alarm as conditions in the  woods get worse every day
 

